From 6da09194095f45b7efece70357a234ba014e52a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JUI YU LIN <hlpmrlinyu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 13:54:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] avcodec/exr: port the PIZ Huffman decoder from OpenEXR

huf_build_dec_table() built a VLC table with ff_vlc_init_sparse(..., nb_bits=12)
and huf_decode() read it back with get_vlc2(). VLC subtable indices are stored
in VLCBaseType, which is int16_t (libavcodec/vlc.h), so as soon as a table needs
a subtable index above 32767, build_table() takes its "strange codes" path and
returns AVERROR_PATCHWELCOME (libavcodec/vlc.c). huf_uncompress() then failed
and the entire 32 line scanline block was left zero filled, which shows up as
horizontal black bands. Only a log line was emitted; decoding otherwise
"succeeded".

Dense tables are easy to reach with 32 bit float content, where a block can
carry tens of thousands of codes longer than 14 bits. On a 1920x1080 PIZ
sequence, 25 of 30 sampled frames failed this way, in the worst case all 34
blocks of a frame.

Replace the VLC table with the reference decoder from OpenEXR's ImfHuf.cpp: a
14 bit primary table plus, for codes longer than 14 bits, a per entry list of
candidate symbols matched against the canonical code. That has no 16 bit index
limit, so dense tables decode. The run length symbol is the pseudo-symbol at
index iM, which is what the encoder emits, rather than a zero frequency symbol
chosen heuristically.

Unlike the reference, the refill inside the run length expansion checks that
the input pointer is still inside the buffer before reading a byte.

The affected frames now decode byte exactly against the OpenEXR reference
library, and the existing fate exr tests are unchanged.

This looks like the same defect as ticket #11486, whose log shows the same
"strange codes" message for a half float PIZ file; that reporter's sample was
not available here, so it is not claimed as fixed.

Signed-off-by: JUI YU LIN <hlpmrlinyu@gmail.com>
